If QGP is formed, the system must quickly expands and cools due to its excess pressure with respect to the vacuum, this evolution can be described by relativistic hydrodynamics. At some point, therefore, \(T\) falls below \(T_{\rm c}\) and hadrons are reformed and the quarks and gluons become confined. If there is a first-order phase transition from quarks and gluons to hadrons, then it will go through a mixed phase, in which quarks and gluons coexist with hadrons. The expansion is likely to be isothermal in this phase and latent heat is used to convert the quarks and gluons to hadrons. As the system of hadrons continues to expand, "chemical freeze-out" occurs at a temperature \(T_{\rm ch}\), where the inelastic interactions between the hadrons cease and the composition of different hadron species do not change any more. The resulting hadronic gas continues to cool until interaction rates become insufficient to

