The STAR is designed to detect charged and neutral particles produced at RHIC. The trigger system looks at every RHIC crossing and decide whether or not to accept that event and initiate the recording of the data. The STAR data acquisition system (DAQ) [36] is fast, flexible, and receives data from multiple detectors which have a wide range of readout rates. The interaction rates at RHIC for the beams of highest luminosity can approach \(\sim\)10 MHz. The major part of the STAR data is provided by the slow detectors such as TPC, FTPC, and EMC (Electro Magnetic Calorimeters), which can operate at rates of \(\sim\)100 Hz

