As the beam energy increases from lower to higher energies, the \(T_{\rm ch}\) increases and chemical potentials decreases. The \(T_{\rm ch}\) increases from lower AGS energies up to higher SPS and lower BES-I energies, then it seems to saturate up to top RHIC energies after that it shows a slightly lower value for LHC energy. While chemical potentials are decreasing with \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\). The trend of chemical potential shows less baryon stopping with increase in \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\). At lower AGS and SPS energies, the original baryons are stopped in the collision and the mid-rapidity region contains most of the net-baryons. Therefore, at lower energies, the chemical potential has a higher value than at higher energies, when the collisions are more transparent and the mid-rapidity region is almost a net-baryon free region. Net-baryon density decreases with increasing collision energies. From all of the above studies, the extracted freeze-out parameters are quite robust and insensitive to the choice of ensembles, fitting procedures and whether or not particles like \(\phi\) and \(\Omega\) are included in the fits. Till now, we have presented all the results within 1CFO. The 1CFO fits of all particle yields studied here in GCE and SCE are given in Appendix B. The \(\chi^{2}/{\rm NDF}\), which is a measure of the goodness of fits, is found to be quite large at few energies. This has led to the development of new freeze-out schemes which can describe the yields better. One such development which we will discuss here is the 2CFO scheme of freeze-out. The freeze-out parameters extracted in the 1CFO scheme and compared with those of 2CFO [47] are shown in Fig. 5.4 in the SCE with \(\gamma_{s}=1\) and \(\mu_{Q}\) solved from Eq. 5.4. We find that the non-strange freeze-out temperature, \(T_{\rm ns}\), is consistently lower than the strange freeze-out temperature, \(T_{\rm s}\), at all the energies. While at the LHC, \(T_{\rm s}\) and \(T_{\rm ns}\) differ by about 5% and at the top SPS energy they differ by about 15%. This can be interpreted as an early freeze-out for the strange hadrons [28, 48]. For all energies, the 1CFO freeze-out parameters lie intermediate to the corresponding 2CFO values for the non-strange and strange CFO surfaces. There is substantial improvement in \(\chi^{2}/{\rm NDF}\) for 2CFO as compared to 1CFO.

Now we will look into the different particle ratios within 1CFO and 2CFO freeze-outschemes. Strange to non-strange particle ratios are particularly sensitive to the choice of CFO scheme as argued in [49]. This is easily understood from the following expression for the particle ratios in statistical model,

\[N_{i}/N_{j} = \frac{g_{i}V_{i}}{g_{j}V_{j}}\left(\frac{T_{i}m_{i}}{T_{j}m_{j}} \right)^{3/2}\exp\left(m_{j}/T_{j}-m_{i}/T_{i}\right)\times \tag{5.6}\] \[\exp\left(B_{i}\mu_{Bi}/T_{i}-B_{j}\mu_{Bj}/T_{j}\right)\times\] \[\exp\left(Q_{i}\mu_{Q_{i}}/T_{i}-Q_{j}\mu_{Q_{j}}/T_{j}\right)\times\] \[\exp\left(S_{i}\mu_{S_{i}}/T_{i}-S_{j}\mu_{S_{j}}/T_{j}\right)\]

The above equation is obtained from Eq. 5.3 by taking the asymptotic limit \(m/T>>1\). It is clearly evident from Eq. 5.6 that for ratios of particles of unlike flavors, the thermal factor coefficient \(\left(V_{i}T_{i}^{3/2}\right)/\left(V_{j}T_{j}^{3/2}\right)\) do not cancel off. In 1CFO this additional factor does not arise at all. Thus ratios of particles of unlike flavor are good probes to distinguish different freeze-out schemes.

