unlike flavor like \(\mathrm{K}/\pi\) and \(\Lambda/\mathrm{p}\) discriminate between different CFO schemes. The 2CFO scheme is found to describe all the ratios better as compared to 1CFO. In bottom right panel of Fig. 5.5, a triple ratio \(\left(\Lambda/\overline{\Lambda}\right)/\left((\mathrm{p}/\bar{\mathrm{p}})( \mathrm{K}^{-}/\mathrm{K}^{+})\right)\) is shown. Since the total \(B\), \(Q\), and \(S\) charges carried by \(\Lambda\) is same as those carried by \(\mathrm{p}\) and \(\mathrm{K}\) together, the fugacity factors cancel off and this ratio is to a very good approximation unity in 1CFO [50]. However, in 2CFO there is no such constraint. We find in data appreciable deviation from unity below \(\sqrt{s_{\mathrm{NN}}}=10\) GeV which can not be described in 1CFO. Currently the errors are very large and hence it is difficult to conclude anything definite. The main reason behind this is that the errors have been computed in quadratures. This might have led to an overestimation of the errors. A proper estimate of the errors of these quantities by taking into account correlations among various sources of errors is highly desired from experimental side. For all the CFO results discussed above, the \(\chi^{2}/\mathrm{NDF}\) vs. \(\sqrt{s_{\mathrm{NN}}}\) is shown in Fig. 5.6.

